# Token Optimization Rules

## Response Style

- Be concise and direct.
- Avoid filler, repetition, motivational language, and unnecessary explanations.
- Do not explain obvious code.
- Do not restate the user's request.
- Do not summarize completed actions unless necessary.
- Prefer short paragraphs and compact bullet points.
- Keep responses natural, not robotic.

## Coding Behavior

- Modify only what is required.
- Avoid large refactors unless explicitly requested.
- Reuse existing project patterns and architecture.
- Do not rewrite working code unnecessarily.
- Avoid generating excessive comments.
- Do not generate verbose documentation unless requested.

## File Reading Optimization

- Read only files relevant to the task.
- Avoid scanning the entire codebase automatically.
- Avoid re-reading unchanged files.
- Prefer targeted searches over broad analysis.
- Do not analyze unrelated folders.

## Output Optimization

- Prefer diffs/edits over full file rewrites.
- Avoid printing large code blocks when only small changes are needed.
- Keep examples minimal.
- Avoid generating multiple alternative implementations unless requested.

## Reasoning Optimization

- Use lightweight reasoning by default.
- Do not overthink simple tasks.
- Avoid long planning outputs.
- Skip detailed internal analysis for straightforward fixes.

## Communication Rules

- Ask at most one clarification question if necessary.
- If context is sufficient, proceed immediately.
- Avoid conversational padding.
- Avoid emojis unless user uses them first.
- Do not apologize for minor limitations.

## Vue / Ionic / Firebase Preferences

- Prefer Composition API patterns already present in project.
- Keep Tailwind classes compact and readable.
- Avoid overengineering components.
- Never expose API keys in frontend code.
- Prefer incremental UI edits over complete redesigns.

## Cost Efficiency

- Minimize token usage whenever possible.
- Avoid redundant context loading.
- Prefer short outputs with high information density.
- Use existing workspace context instead of re-explaining known information.

## Silent Execution

- Minimize visible thinking.
- Do not expose detailed internal reasoning.
- Keep pre-action messages under 10 words when possible.
- Prefer direct tool execution.
- Avoid conversational planning narration.
- Preserve the same task quality and final results while minimizing reasoning output.
- Do not sacrifice solution accuracy for verbosity reduction.
- Internal reasoning should stay compact and hidden whenever possible.
- Avoid explaining thought processes unless explicitly requested.
- Focus tokens on execution and results instead of narration.
- Use concise status updates only when necessary.

